UNIT rust_carried_table
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/v05.mjs rustCarriedTable (with rustTokens, rustValue, castOf, rustEntries, carriedShapeType) — the reader that decides what a Rust carried-data module's table hashes to; reproduced exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG rust_carried_table(src: text, shape: text | none) -> map[text, json]
ENVELOPE src is well-formed text, usually Rust source; the reader never compiles it, so any text is in scope as long as
  every escape it holds obeys R4's in-scope forms (a "\x" escape followed by two hex digits; a "\u{…}" escape whose HEX is
  empty, all hex digits, or starts with a character R4 says throws) and no escape names a surrogate code point
  (U+D800..U+DFFF), and no value R6 reads nests deeper than 50 sequences. shape is none, the empty text, or any text (R10
  and R11 say which texts throw). Throws are IN scope as observations: R3, R4, R6, R7, R9, R10, R11, R12 and R13 say where
  the reader throws; the message is never graded.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Read a Rust carried-data module as the (key, value) table it declares. The text is cut into tokens (R1–R5);
  entries are found in the token stream either as ("key", value) tuples or, failing any tuple, as "key" "=>" value arms
  (R6–R9); an optional declared SHAPE (an abstract map type) checks every key and value (R10–R12); a repeated key must carry
  the same value (R13). The result is the table as a map from key text to a json value: text, number, bool, none, or a list
  of those, nested.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Tokens. src is read left to right as a sequence of characters; at each position the FIRST matching row of TABLE LEX
  applies, then reading continues after what it consumed. Whitespace and comments make no token.
TABLE LEX — what starts at the current position
  space, tab, CR or LF                                                                     | skipped
  two slashes                                                                              | a LINE COMMENT, skipped up to (not including) the next LF, or to the end of src
  slash then star                                                                          | a BLOCK COMMENT, skipped: depth starts at 1; "/*" adds one, "*/" subtracts one (each consumes two characters); it ends when depth is 0 — or silently at the end of src when it never closes
  an optional "b", then "r", then zero or more hash marks, then a double quote — all within the next 32 characters | a RAW STRING: STR token, its text is everything up to the first double quote followed by the same number of hash marks, verbatim (no escapes); none such -> throws
  a double quote, or "b" then a double quote                                               | a STRING: STR token, R3
  an apostrophe                                                                            | a CHAR literal when R2 accepts: CHR token; otherwise the punctuation token APOSTROPHE (a lifetime or label)
  an ASCII digit                                                                           | a NUMBER: NUM token, R5
  an ASCII letter or underscore                                                            | a WORD: the longest run of ASCII letters, digits and underscores; "true" / "false" are BOOL tokens, any other run an IDENT token
  "=>", "->", "::" or ".."                                                                 | that two-character punctuation token
  any other character                                                                      | a one-character punctuation token (a non-ASCII character outside a literal included; "||" is two "|" tokens)
R2 CHAR literal: an apostrophe, then exactly one of — a single character that is neither an apostrophe nor a backslash
  (any code point, LF included); or a backslash followed by one of n r t 0 \ ' or the double quote; or "\x" and exactly two
  hex digits; or "\u{" then 1 to 8 characters each a hex digit or underscore, then "}" — then a closing apostrophe. Anything
  else (a lifetime such as 'a or 'static, a two-character body) is not a char literal. The body is decoded by R4.
R3 STRING: after the opening double quote (and its "b"), the body runs to the next double quote not consumed by an escape —
  a backslash always consumes itself and the ONE character after it. No closing quote before the end of src -> throws. The
  body is decoded by R4. A byte string is read as text exactly like a string.
R4 ESCAPES (strings and char bodies), by TABLE ESCAPE; every other character stands for itself. A bad or unknown escape
  anywhere in src — inside any string, not only a table entry — -> throws; escapes inside comments are never read.
TABLE ESCAPE — backslash followed by
  n, r, t, 0                    | LF, CR, tab, U+0000
  backslash, apostrophe, double quote | that character
  x and two hex digits          | the character whose code is those two digits (0x00..0xFF; "\xFF" is U+00FF, never a byte)
  u, then "{"                   | HEX = the text up to the next "}" in the body, underscores removed. No "}" -> throws; HEX empty, or starting with a character that is not a hex digit, a TRIM character, "+" or "-" -> throws; HEX all hex digits -> the code point it names, a value above U+10FFFF -> throws
  LF or CR                      | a LINE CONTINUATION: nothing, and every following space, tab, CR and LF is skipped
  anything else (u without "{" included) | throws
PINNED set TRIM = { U+0009, U+000A, U+000B, U+000C, U+000D, U+0020, U+00A0, U+1680, U+2000..U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F, U+3000, U+FEFF } CITED R4 — the ECMAScript WhiteSpace + LineTerminator set (U+FEFF in, U+0085 out); it only separates a throwing "\u{…}" start from an out-of-domain one
R5 NUMBER: the longest prefix, within the next 64 characters, of the form BODY then an optional SUFFIX, where BODY is
  "0x" then one or more of hex digits and underscores, or "0o" then octal digits and underscores, or "0b" then binary digits
  and underscores, or else DECIMAL = an ASCII digit then digits and underscores, an optional fraction ("." then a digit,
  then digits and underscores), an optional exponent (e or E, an optional sign, a digit, then digits and underscores); and
  SUFFIX is an optional underscore then one of u8 u16 u32 u64 u128 usize i8 i16 i32 i64 i128 isize f32 f64. The radix
  prefixes are lowercase only ("0X10" is the number 0 then the word X10). The hex digits of BODY absorb a following f32 or
  f64 ("0x1f64" is the hex value 0x1F64 = 8036). A numeral longer than 64 characters is cut there; the rest starts a new
  token. A "." not followed by a digit ends the numeral ("1." is NUM 1 then the punctuation "."). VALUE: drop every
  underscore and the suffix. An INTEGER-SHAPED body (a radix prefix with at least one digit, or decimal digits with no
  fraction and no exponent — whatever the suffix, f32 / f64 included) names an integer N: the literal is EXACT when N is
  exactly a double (N is 0, or N = m × 2^k with m odd, m < 2^53 and N < 2^1024 — so 2^53, 2^60 and 2^64 are exact, 2^53+1
  and 2^64-1 are not), and its value is N. Any other body (a fraction or an exponent) has the nearest double as its value,
  correctly rounded ("9007199254740993.0" is 2^53); it is INEXACT only when that is infinite ("1e400"). A radix prefix left
  with no digit ("0x_") is INEXACT. An f32 SUFFIX on a decimal, octal or binary body (never hex: there it is digits) narrows:
  the literal is ALSO INEXACT when its value is not exactly a finite IEEE-754 binary32 value ("0.1f32", "16777217f32",
  "1e-50f32", "1e39f32" and "3.4028235e38f32" are INEXACT; "0.5f32", "16777216f32", "0b1f32" are exact), so an f32 literal
  that reads keeps its value. An INEXACT literal is harmless until R6 reads it as a value; then it throws. CITED FIDELITY
  package — exactness is judged per entry value
R6 VALUE — a table value starting at a token, by TABLE VALUE (first matching row); when no row applies there is no value. A
  value is read left to right and stops at the first token where it fails; a throw (an INEXACT literal, a CAST) happens
  when reading reaches that token, even inside a candidate R7 then rejects ("(\"a\", 99999999999999999 + 1)" throws by R6,
  "(\"a\", 1 + 99999999999999999)" does not: reading stopped at "+" before the numeral).
TABLE VALUE — the token(s) at the position
  punctuation "&"                         | the value after it (any number of "&" are skipped)
  STR, CHR or BOOL                        | its text / text / bool
  NUM                                     | its value (INEXACT -> throws), then TABLE CAST
  punctuation "-" then NUM                | the negated value (INEXACT -> throws; negating 0 gives -0), then TABLE CAST
  IDENT "None"                            | none
  IDENT "Some", "(" , IDENT Some or None  | throws (a nested Option: "Some(None)", "Some(Some(1))", "Some(Some)") — only when that IDENT is the very next token: "Some(&None)" and "Some((None))" are none, "Some(&Some(1))" is 1
  IDENT "Some", "(" , a value, ")"        | that value (nothing else may stand inside)
  "[" or "("                              | a SEQUENCE: values separated by ",", an optional trailing ",", closed by the matching "]" or ")". A list of the items, except that "(" with exactly one item and no comma is that item itself ("(1)" is 1, "(1,)" is [1], "()" is []). Any item without a value, or anything but "," or the closer after an item -> no value
  anything else                           | no value ("vec!", "b'a'", "c\"x\"", a block, "1 + 2")
TABLE CAST — after a number value x, by the tokens that follow (at most one cast is read; "1 as u8 as u16" leaves the second "as" after the value, so its candidate is rejected)
  not IDENT "as"                          | x, unchanged
  "as" then anything but an IDENT         | throws
  "as" f64                                | x
  "as" f32                                | x when x is exactly an IEEE-754 binary32 value (finite); otherwise throws ("0.1 as f32")
  "as" u8 u16 u32 i8 i16 i32              | x when x is an integer (no fractional part; -0, 1.0 and 1e3 are integers) within the type's range (u8 0..255, u16 0..65535, u32 0..4294967295, i8 -128..127, i16 -32768..32767, i32 -2147483648..2147483647); otherwise throws ("255 as i8", "1.9 as u8", "-1 as u8")
  "as" u64 usize u128                     | x when an integer within 0..2^53 (2^53 included); otherwise throws ("18014398509481984 as u64", though that literal alone reads)
  "as" i64 isize i128                     | x when an integer within -2^53..2^53; otherwise throws
  (every integer-cast row above)          | a -0 that passes gives 0 ("-0 as i64", "-0.0 as u8" are 0); "as f64" / "as f32" keep -0, and an uncast "-0" or "-0i64" stays -0
  "as" any other IDENT                    | throws ("1 as char", "1 as U8"); a cast after a non-number ("true as u8", "'a' as u8", "(1) as u8") is no cast: that value ends before "as"
R7 ENTRY scan: the token stream is scanned from the first token. At each token, in this order: (a) TUPLE candidate — the
  token is "(" followed by a STR token K and ",", a value V starts after the comma, and the token after V is ")": the entry
  (K, V) joins the TUPLES and the scan resumes after that ")"; when NO value starts after the comma (an IDENT, "b'a'", a
  sequence with an item that has none, the end of src) -> throws; when a value starts but the token after it is not ")"
  ("(\"a\", 1 + 2)", "(\"a\", true as u8)", "(\"a\", 1, 2)") the candidate is silently rejected and the scan moves one
  token on; (b) OR-PATTERN — the token is a STR token, the next is the punctuation
  "|" and the one after it is a STR token -> throws ("s == \"a\" || t" and "\"a\" | x" do not); (c) ARM candidate — the
  token is a STR token K followed by "=>": when a value V starts after it and the token after V is "," or "}", the entry
  (K, V) joins the ARMS and the scan resumes at that "," or "}" token; otherwise (no value, or anything else after it, the
  end of src included: "\"a\" => 1" alone) -> throws. Otherwise the scan moves one token on. Tokens inside a value an
  accepted candidate consumed are never visited; tokens of a rejected candidate are: "(\"a\", (\"b\", 1), 3)" yields only
  the entry b: 1. The scan covers the WHOLE token stream, and its throws fire even when R8 later discards the form: any call
  shaped ("text", value) anywhere in src is a tuple entry, a ("text", name) call such as format!("{}", n) throws, and so does
  a "text" => arm whose value is not one R6 reads. CITED FIDELITY package
R8 FORM: when TUPLES is non-empty the entries are TUPLES, in order, and ARMS are discarded entirely; otherwise the entries
  are ARMS, in order. A single stray tuple therefore replaces a whole arm table. CITED FIDELITY package
R9 No entries -> throws.
R10 SHAPE given: shape is none or the empty text -> no shape check (R11–R12 skipped). Otherwise the DECLARED TYPE is read
  from shape by R11; when it is not a map type -> throws.
R11 Reading shape. WS = space, tab, CR, LF (the grammar's own whitespace; never VT, FF, NBSP or other Unicode space).
  (1) keep the text before its first ";"; (2) NORM: remove WS from both ends; when the result is "map", then one or more
  WS, then K, optional WS, "->", optional WS, V — K the SHORTEST non-empty piece for which such a split exists, V the rest
  (at least one character) — the text becomes "map[" NORM(K) ", " NORM(V) "]"; otherwise it stays as it is; (3) WIDTH:
  every u8, u16, u32 standing as a TYPE becomes u64 and every i8, i16, i32 so standing becomes i64 — standing as a type
  means: at the start of the text or right after one of "[" "," "|" ":" (WS between allowed), and followed (WS between
  allowed) by "]", ",", "|" or the end of the text ("record[u8: u8]" renames only the field's type; "map[text,\nu8]" is
  renamed); (4) the result is read by GRAMMAR TYPE over the whole text; a text it does not accept -> throws (a shape of
  only WS reads as the empty text, which throws).
PINNED grammar TYPE = alt ("|" alt)* CITED FIDELITY package — the shape is parsed by the codec's one type reader (canon.mjs parseType)
  alt = one of the words text bytes bool none i64 u64 f64 json, or tuple[T ("," T)*], list[T], map[T "," T], or
  record[name ":" T ("," name ":" T)*] with unique names; name = an ASCII letter or underscore then ASCII letters, digits,
  underscores. Between any two tokens space, tab, CR or LF may stand (optional; required only between two words); no other
  character may stand anywhere outside a token (a leading NBSP, VT or U+FEFF throws).
  Composite types (tuple, list, map, record — the shape's own map counted) nest at most 16 deep; 17 throws. A union is
  refused (throws) when a later alternative is COVERED by an earlier one, by TABLE COVERS.
TABLE COVERS — an earlier alternative A covers a later B when any row holds
  same type                     | A and B are the same type, unions compared as SETS of alternatives and records as SETS of named fields (tuples in order)
  json                          | A is json and B is JSON-ACCEPTED: none, bool, f64, text or json; a list, or a tuple, of JSON-ACCEPTED parts; a map with a text key and a JSON-ACCEPTED value; a record whose fields are all JSON-ACCEPTED; a union of JSON-ACCEPTED alternatives
  pointwise                     | both lists and A's item covers B's; both tuples of one length, each A item covering B's at its place; both maps, A's key covering B's key and A's value covering B's value
  record                        | both records and every field of A has a field of B with the same name that it covers (B may have more fields)
  union                         | A is a union and one of its alternatives covers B; or B is a union and A covers every alternative of B
R12 FIT, for every entry in order under the declared type map[KT, VT]: the key text must fit KT and the value must fit VT, by
  TABLE FIT; a misfit -> throws.
TABLE FIT — a reader value v against a type T
  text              | v is text
  bool              | v is a bool
  none              | v is none
  f64               | v is a number
  i64 / u64         | v is a number with no fractional part ("1.0", "1e3" and -0 fit) within -2^63..2^63-1 / 0..2^64-1, compared exactly ("1e19" fits u64, "9.3e18" misfits i64)
  json              | always
  list[I]           | v is a list and every item fits I
  tuple[T1..Tn]     | v is a list of exactly n items, item k fitting Tk
  union             | v fits at least one alternative
  bytes, map[…], record[…] | never (the reader yields no bytes and no map)
R13 DUPLICATES, in entry order, building the result: an entry whose key is already a key of the result must carry the SAME
  value, else -> throws. SAME: two non-lists are the same when they are one value (one text, one bool, both none, or one
  number where -0 and 0 DIFFER); an earlier list is the same as a later list with the same items at every depth where,
  inside a list, -0 and 0 are equal; a list and a non-list differ. When the same, the later value replaces the earlier ([0]
  then [-0] leaves [-0]). Every key is ordinary text: "constructor", "__proto__", "toString" are keys like any other.
R14 The result: every key of the entries with its value from R13. Key order is immaterial (the canonical form sorts).
INVARIANT every number in the result is finite and exactly the double R5 and R6 gave it; the result is never empty.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN what the reader does outside ENVELOPE (not reproduced; no vector reaches it): a "\x" escape whose next two
  characters are not both hex digits yields a character from a lenient host integer read (a non-hex start gives U+0000, a
  single trailing digit stands alone, "\x 1" reads 1); any other "\u{…}" HEX is read leniently — leading TRIM characters
  skipped, an optional sign, an optional "0x", then the longest hex prefix ("\u{1g}" and "\u{ 0x1}" are U+0001, a negative
  value throws, "\u{-0}" is U+0000); an escape naming a surrogate gives text that is not well-formed (a shape check of it
  throws); a value nested past the codec's value cap (100) throws in a shape check, and far deeper exhausts the host stack.
ORACLE value -> oracles/rust_carried_table.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 29a519c892f505096e614bfbd8bb758dfd7a057c26e0cd97dc9bbd7bb5c6dff2
SCOPE IN: R1–R14 over ENVELOPE. OUT: OUT-OF-DOMAIN; the error message text (only throw-ness is graded); the file name the
  reader puts in its messages; the line numbers it records per entry.
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (rederive, the author's own repository), cli/sir/v05.mjs: rustCarriedTable, rustTokens,
  rustUnescape, rustNumber, RUST_CHAR, RUST_NUM, rustValue, castOf, exact, rustEntries, carriedShapeType; cli/sir/canon.mjs:
  parseType, covers, inJson, encode. Round-5b delta re-cut 2026-10-27 from the pinned authority snapshot, seam
  selfhost/seams/rust_carried_table.mjs.
